silience, decision quality), sees complex connections (technical), excellent spatial vision (technical), very good leadership (crew cooperation). Concerns: not good at taking criticism (cooperation/adaptability), unorganized (attention/accuracy). So mixed.\n\nCandidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately (stress resilience), concentrates well (attention), solves problems extremely well (technical), takes responsibility seriously (responsibility). Concerns: arrogant (crew cooperation), weak leadership (crew cooperation). So also concerns.\n\nWe need to compare. Which candidate best fits? Candidate B seems strong on reliability and stress, but interpersonal concerns. Candidate C strong on situational awareness and leadership but issues with criticism and organization. Candidate D strong on problem solving and responsibility but arrogance. Candidate A strong on reliability and crew atmosphere but communication difficulty and egocentric.\n\nWe might argue that Candidate B's interpersonal issues (grumpy, uncooperative) could be more detrimental than Candidate A's communication difficulty? Both are concerning. But Candidate A also has egocentric and not willing to further education, which is a big adaptability issue. Candidate B's issues may be mitigated.\n\nThus we might agree with Agent 2 that Candidate B is currently the best, but we need to ask about crew cooperation for B. We could ask Agent 2 for more detail on B's uncooperative behavior. But we can also bring up Candidate C's strengths and concerns.\n\nWe need to produce a public message with our vote. Let's say we still support Candidate B, but we want more info on B's crew cooperation. Or we could shift to Candidate C if we think leadership and anticipation outweigh B's interpersonal issues. But we need to be systematic.\n\nGiven the current leading candidate is B, we can either support or challenge. Let's support but ask for clarification on B's uncooperative behavior and its impact. Also note that Candidate C's leadership is strong but unorganized and not good at criticism, which could be problematic for long-distance flights.\n\nThus we can say: I agree with Agent 2's provisional recommendation of Candidate B, given his high reliability and stress resilience, but we need to examine his crew cooperation more closely. I also want to compare with Candidate C's leadership and situational awareness.\n\nWe need to include explicit facts: B is very conscientious, handles stress well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills; also can be grumpy, uncooperative. C anticipates dangerous situations, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership; sometimes not good at taking criticism, can be unorganized.\n\nWe need to state our vote: Candidate B.\n\nLet's produce the public message."}
